@@ -0,0 +1,152 @@
+# StackChan patch: esp-ml307 disconnect-callback race fix
+
+## What this is
+
+This repo (`78/esp-ml307`, normally fetched by ESP-IDF's Component Manager
+into `firmware/managed_components/78__esp-ml307/`, gitignored, pinned by
+`firmware/main/idf_component.yml`'s `78/esp-ml307: ~3.6.4` /
+`firmware/main/dependencies.lock`) is ALSO vendored a second way in this
+project: cloned directly by `firmware/fetch_repos.py` (per the entry in
+`firmware/repos.json`) into `firmware/components/78__esp-ml307/`, at the
+exact commit the Component Manager resolved
+(`ab4de7c28c8b8f809eba2f56f38090d57fce984d`, i.e. what `~3.6.4` currently
+means in practice - see `repository_info.commit_sha` in the managed copy's
+own `idf_component.yml`), with this project's one patch
+(`firmware/patches/esp-ml307.patch`) applied on top by that same script -
+exactly the same pattern already used for `firmware/xiaozhi-esp32/` and its
+`firmware/patches/xiaozhi-esp32.patch`.
+
+**Why this exists / why not just edit `managed_components/` directly:**
+`managed_components/` is entirely owned by the Component Manager and
+gitignored - any hand-edit there is silently lost on the next clean clone,
+`idf.py fullclean`, or dependency re-resolution, with no safety net.
+
+**Why this overrides the Component-Manager-managed copy at build time:**
+ESP-IDF's documented component search precedence (highest to lowest) is:
+project `components/` -> `EXTRA_COMPONENT_DIRS` -> `managed_components/` ->
+`IDF_PATH/components`. A project component directory with the same name
+(`78__esp-ml307`) automatically wins over the managed copy, no extra
+CMake/YAML configuration needed - confirmed against ESP-IDF's official
+build-system docs before relying on this, not assumed. `managed_components/
+78__esp-ml307/` still gets fetched and still exists on disk alongside this
+one; it's simply shadowed and never compiled while this directory is
+present.
+
+**How this directory gets (re)created:** `python3 firmware/fetch_repos.py`
+clones `https://github.com/78/esp-ml307.git` at the commit above into
+`firmware/components/78__esp-ml307/` and applies
+`firmware/patches/esp-ml307.patch` via `git apply` - same mechanism as
+every other entry in `firmware/repos.json`. This directory itself is
+gitignored (`firmware/.gitignore`'s `/components/` line) and is expected to
+be absent on a fresh clone until `fetch_repos.py` runs.
+
+## What the patch changes vs. upstream commit `ab4de7c`
+
+Exactly two files, both under `src/esp/` (the WiFi/`EspNetwork` backend -
+the only backend this board actually uses; this same package's `ec801e/`
+and `ml307/` cellular-modem backends are compiled out for the `esp32`
+target and irrelevant to this project):
+
+- **`src/esp/esp_tcp.h`** - added `#include <atomic>` and one new private
+  member, `std::atomic<bool> disconnect_callback_fired_{false};`, on
+  `EspTcp`.
+- **`src/esp/esp_tcp.cc`** - two changes:
+  1. `Connect()`: resets `disconnect_callback_fired_` to `false` right
+     after `connected_ = true;`, so a fresh connection starts with a clean
+     guard.
+  2. `DoDisconnect(bool wait_for_task)`: the final
+     `if (disconnect_callback_) { disconnect_callback_(); }` is now gated
+     by `disconnect_callback_fired_.compare_exchange_strong(expected, true, ...)`
+     - only the first task to reach that line for a given connection's
+     lifetime actually invokes the callback.
+
+Plus this file itself (new, not present upstream).
+
+## The bug this fixes - full incident writeup
+
+**Symptom on hardware:** after a successful ASR -> `/stackChan/device/chat`
+-> TTS round trip in `hal_module_llm.cpp`'s new `chat_with_agent()`
+(milestone 2 full wire-up), the ESP32 would reliably crash right as the
+HTTP connection to the self-hosted server (CT141, `192.168.1.41:12800`)
+tore down. Under marginal USB power (a laptop port) this manifested as a
+**silent, total hang** - screen dark, USB gone, no panic text, unrecoverable
+without a full power cycle of the whole stack. Once powered through a
+powered USB hub (ruling out power as the actual cause - the crash still
+happened, but this time the board had enough headroom to complete its
+panic-print-and-reboot sequence and recover on its own), the real fault
+printed cleanly:
+
+```
+Guru Meditation Error: Core  0 panic'ed (Interrupt wdt timeout on CPU0).
+...
+Backtrace: ... esp_cpu_compare_and_set ... spinlock_acquire ... xQueueSemaphoreTake
+  ... pthread_mutex_lock ... std::mutex::lock() ... std::lock_guard<std::mutex>::lock_guard
+  ... HttpClient::OnTcpDisconnected() at .../http_client.cc:279
+  ... HttpClient::Open(...)::{lambda()#1}::_M_invoke at .../http_client.cc:212
+  ... EspTcp::DoDisconnect(bool) at .../esp_tcp.cc:102
+  ... EspTcp::ReceiveTask() at .../esp_tcp.cc:140
+  ... EspTcp::Connect(...)::{lambda(void*)#1}::_FUN at .../esp_tcp.cc:66
+```
+
+**Root cause, traced from this exact source (not guessed):**
+
+`chat_with_agent()` creates a short-lived `Http` object, calls
+`Open()`/`GetStatusCode()`/`ReadAll()`, and lets it fall out of scope at
+function return. `~HttpClient()` then calls `Close()` ->
+`tcp_->Disconnect()` -> `EspTcp::DoDisconnect(true)` (an *active* close, on
+our own "asr_chat" worker task): it closes the socket fd, then blocks (up
+to 10s) waiting for the background `"tcp_receive"` task to signal it has
+exited, and only then invokes `disconnect_callback_()` (==
+`HttpClient::OnTcpDisconnected()`, which takes `std::lock_guard<mutex_>`).
+
+But CT141's Go/GoFrame backend closes its end of the connection right after
+writing the response (no keep-alive), so the background `"tcp_receive"`
+task's own blocked `recv()` call is *also*, independently and concurrently,
+about to fail - and when it does, `ReceiveTask()` calls
+`DoDisconnect(false)` (a *passive* close) itself, which **also**
+unconditionally invokes `disconnect_callback_()` - from a second task,
+racing the first.
+
+Nothing in `EspTcp` (`connected_`, `tcp_fd_`, `disconnect_callback_`) was
+synchronized before this patch - confirmed by reading `esp_tcp.h`/`.cc` in
+full, not assumed. Two tasks concurrently entering
+`HttpClient::OnTcpDisconnected()`'s non-reentrant-safe locking is what the
+panic's backtrace shows directly.
+
+**This is a pre-existing bug in the upstream component**, not something
+introduced by this project's new code - `main/hal/hal_account.cpp`'s two
+`http->ReadAll()` call sites use the identical implicit-destructor pattern
+and are equally exposed; they simply haven't hit a server whose close
+timing opens this exact race window. `chat_with_agent()` is just the first
+caller in this codebase to reliably trigger it, because of how CT141
+happens to close its connections.
+
+**Scope of the fix:** intentionally narrow. It stops the one thing that
+was actively harmful - the non-reentrant callback firing twice - via an
+atomic compare-and-swap guard. It does **not** make the rest of the class
+fully thread-safe (`connected_`/`tcp_fd_` are still touched non-atomically
+from two tasks); those writes happen to be idempotent (both tasks would
+only ever write the same `false`/`-1`), so they weren't the source of the
+observed crash and were left alone to keep this patch minimal and easy to
+reason about.
+
+## Maintaining this override
+
+- **Verifying it's actually being used:** during a build, check that
+  `esp_tcp.cc.obj`/build log references trace back to
+  `firmware/components/78__esp-ml307/...`, not
+  `firmware/managed_components/78__esp-ml307/...`.
+- **If `main/idf_component.yml`'s pinned version (`~3.6.4`) is ever
+  bumped:** this override will keep pinning the OLD commit
+  (`ab4de7c28c8b8f809eba2f56f38090d57fce984d`) via `repos.json` and will
+  NOT automatically track the newer managed version. Before bumping,
+  check `78/esp-ml307`'s upstream history for whether this exact race has
+  since been fixed there. If so: remove this entry from `repos.json`,
+  delete `firmware/components/78__esp-ml307/` and
+  `firmware/patches/esp-ml307.patch`, and let the Component Manager
+  resolve the newer version normally. If not: update the `branch` field
+  in `repos.json` to the new commit/tag, re-run `fetch_repos.py`, and
+  confirm `git apply --check firmware/patches/esp-ml307.patch` still
+  applies cleanly against the new commit (re-generate the patch if it
+  doesn't - the two changes described above are small enough to reapply
+  by hand against the new source if needed).
diff --git a/src/esp/esp_tcp.cc b/src/esp/esp_tcp.cc
index 14a1719..8a0af5b 100644
--- a/src/esp/esp_tcp.cc
+++ b/src/esp/esp_tcp.cc
@@ -59,6 +59,10 @@ bool EspTcp::Connect(const std::string& host, int port) {
     }
 
     connected_ = true;
+    // StackChan local override: fresh guard for this connection's lifetime
+    // - see the members-block comment in esp_tcp.h and
+    // STACKCHAN_OVERRIDE.md for why this exists.
+    disconnect_callback_fired_.store(false, std::memory_order_relaxed);
 
     xEventGroupClearBits(event_group_, ESP_TCP_EVENT_RECEIVE_TASK_EXIT);
     xTaskCreate([](void* arg) {
@@ -98,7 +102,48 @@ void EspTcp::DoDisconnect(bool wait_for_task) {
     }
 
     // 断开连接时触发断开回调
-    if (disconnect_callback_) {
+    //
+    // StackChan local override (2026-09-09, Jonathan/Claude - see
+    // STACKCHAN_OVERRIDE.md in this component's root for the full
+    // incident writeup): DoDisconnect() can be entered concurrently by
+    // TWO different tasks for the SAME connection -
+    //   1) the task that owns the HttpClient, via Disconnect() ->
+    //      DoDisconnect(true) (e.g. HttpClient's destructor/Close()
+    //      running right after a successful ReadAll()), and
+    //   2) this object's own background "tcp_receive" task, via
+    //      ReceiveTask() -> DoDisconnect(false), independently noticing
+    //      the same socket died (recv() <= 0) - which happens whenever
+    //      the peer closes its end at roughly the same time as #1, or
+    //      simply because #1 already called close(tcp_fd_) out from
+    //      under this task's blocked recv() call.
+    // Both paths reach this exact call unconditionally. Without a guard,
+    // disconnect_callback_() - which is HttpClient::OnTcpDisconnected(),
+    // taking a std::lock_guard<std::mutex> on HttpClient::mutex_ - gets
+    // invoked twice, from two different tasks, for one logical
+    // disconnect event. Confirmed on hardware: this raced against
+    // hal_module_llm.cpp's chat_with_agent() (new in this project, the
+    // first caller in this codebase to hit the exact timing window -
+    // CT141's Go backend closes its end right after writing the
+    // response, unlike the servers other Http callers in this project
+    // talk to) and produced a real ESP32 crash:
+    //   Guru Meditation Error: Core 0 panic'ed (Interrupt wdt timeout on CPU0)
+    //   ... HttpClient::OnTcpDisconnected() at http_client.cc:279
+    //   ... EspTcp::ReceiveTask() at esp_tcp.cc:140
+    // This is a PRE-EXISTING, un-guarded race in the upstream component
+    // (confirmed: connected_, tcp_fd_, and disconnect_callback_ have no
+    // synchronization anywhere in this class before this patch) - other
+    // callers in this codebase (hal_account.cpp) use the same
+    // implicit-destructor pattern and are equally exposed; they most
+    // likely just haven't hit a server that closes its end quickly
+    // enough to open this exact race window.
+    //
+    // Fix: only the FIRST task to reach this point for a given
+    // connection's lifetime actually invokes the callback; a concurrent
+    // second caller becomes a silent no-op instead of racing on shared
+    // state.
+    bool expected = false;
+    if (disconnect_callback_ &&
+        disconnect_callback_fired_.compare_exchange_strong(expected, true, std::memory_order_acq_rel)) {
         disconnect_callback_();
     }
 }
